{"name":"Fuchsia Berries","hex":"#333322","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fuchsia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A popular garden plant, of the genus Fuchsia, of the Onagraceae family, shrubs with red, pink or purple flowers.","A purplish-red colour, the color of fuchsin, an aniline dye."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a purplish-red colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fuchsia"},{"term":"berries","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of berry"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of berry"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berries"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fuchsia","blocks":[{"text":"Fuchsia is a genus of flowering plants that consists mostly of shrubs or small trees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Brincos_De_Princesa.jpg/330px-Brincos_De_Princesa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuchsia","wikidata":"Q161066"},{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry","wikidata":"Q16128920"},{"title":"Fuchsia (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Fuchsia is a vivid pinkish–red color, named after the color of the flower of the fuchsia plant, which was named by a French bota